WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.000
Choosing between Type I, Type II, or a staged path is a practical decision.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.740
There is no single correct path for every organization.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:14.320
The choice depends on customer expectations, program maturity, sales needs, risk, budget,

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:16.700
timeline, auditor readiness,

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:20.680
and the organization's ability to operate controls consistently over time.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.940
A first-time SOC 2 program may start with Type I.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.000
This can be useful when the organization needs to validate control design,

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:29.980
build customer confidence,

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:34.520
and create a structured milestone before committing to a full Type II review period.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:39.940
Type I can help the team learn the audit process, refine scope, clarify control owners,

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.520
and identify gaps before the evidence burden increases.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:48.380
Type II is often more valuable for customers who want ongoing assurance.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.640
Enterprise customers, regulated customers,

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:57.060
or mature vendor risk teams may prefer or require Type II because it demonstrates

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.200
that controls operated over time.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:04.620
A Type II report can be stronger evidence that the organization has moved beyond policy

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.760
design into operational

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:07.860
discipline.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.440
Some organizations use Type I as a stepping stone.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:15.180
They complete Type I to confirm design and point-in-time readiness,

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.720
then begin the Type II review period.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.660
This can be a reasonable path when the organization is building its program while also

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.320
responding to customer

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:25.820
assurance needs.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:31.320
The key is to avoid treating Type I as the permanent end state if customers or risks

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:32.500
require

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.800
operating effectiveness evidence.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.980
Other organizations may go directly to Type II if their controls are already mature,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.960
evidence workflows are stable,

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.260
and customer expectations are clear.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:48.880
This path can work when the organization has operated controls for long enough and can

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.940
produce evidence consistently.

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:56.440
It can be risky if control ownership is unclear or evidence collection is still manual and

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:57.700
inconsistent.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.760
The decision should be made honestly.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:04.780
Do not choose Type II only because it sounds better if the program cannot support it.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.600
Do not choose Type I while implying that it proves months of operation.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.380
Define scope, assess readiness,

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.440
talk with qualified advisors and auditors at an educational level,

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.660
understand customer requirements,

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:19.960
and choose the path that matches reality.
